::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which first step best supports professional communication planning?
{
=A. Identify the audience, purpose, and context before choosing tone or format.#Correct. Professional communication begins with audience and purpose because those choices shape the entire message.
~B. Choose the most decorative format first.#This focuses on appearance before communication need. Format should follow audience, purpose, and context.
~C. Write one message for every audience without changes.#This ignores audience awareness. Different audiences may need different details or tone.
~D. Avoid planning so the message sounds natural.#Planning supports clarity. Professional communication should be purposeful, not accidental.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
In an interview or discussion, which response best shows active listening?
{
~A. Change the topic before the speaker finishes.#This does not show listening. It interrupts the communication and ignores the speaker's idea.
~B. Disagree immediately without explanation.#Disagreement can be respectful, but it should respond to the actual idea and include reasoning.
~C. Prepare your answer without listening to the speaker.#This prevents understanding. Active listening requires attention to the speaker's message.
=D. Paraphrase the speaker's point and ask a focused follow-up question.#Correct. This shows that the listener understood the idea and wants to continue the discussion productively.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which multimedia planning choice best supports a presentation?
{
~A. Copy the full script onto every slide.#This overloads the audience and weakens the speaker's role. Slides should support spoken explanation.
~B. Add unrelated pictures for decoration.#Unrelated visuals distract from the message. Multimedia should clarify the presentation.
=C. Use clear headings, relevant visuals, and speaker notes that explain key points.#Correct. These choices help the audience follow the presentation while the speaker explains details.
~D. Remove organization so the presentation feels flexible.#Flexibility does not replace structure. Audiences need a clear path through the message.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
What makes a professional briefing research-supported?
{
~A. It includes a source title without explanation.#A title alone does not support reasoning. The speaker must explain the evidence.
=B. It uses relevant evidence and explains how that evidence supports the recommendation.#Correct. Research-supported communication connects source evidence to the message and audience purpose.
~C. It avoids evidence so the recommendation sounds direct.#Directness is useful, but recommendations are stronger when evidence supports them.
~D. It uses only personal preference.#Preference alone is not research support. A briefing needs relevant source evidence.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Which inference is most careful?
{
=A. This survey suggests that this audience may prefer shorter slides.#Correct. This stays close to the evidence and avoids claiming more than the source supports.
~B. This survey proves every audience hates long slides.#This overclaims. A limited survey cannot prove what every audience prefers.
~C. This survey means slides do not matter.#This contradicts the idea of using evidence to guide communication choices.
~D. This survey should be ignored because all surveys are useless.#This is too extreme. A source can be useful when its limits are explained.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which digital publication choice best supports audience use?
{
~A. Tiny text, low contrast, and hidden details.#This makes the publication hard to use. Digital communication should be readable and accessible.
=B. Clear headings, readable contrast, and a visible next step.#Correct. These choices help the audience scan the message and know what action to take.
~C. Many decorations with no purpose statement.#Decoration alone does not communicate. The audience needs clear meaning and action information.
~D. A format chosen only because it is new.#A new platform is not automatically useful. The format must fit the audience and purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
How should visuals work in a spoken presentation?
{
~A. They should replace the speaker's explanation.#Visuals support communication, but the speaker still needs to explain the message.
~B. They should be unrelated so the audience stays surprised.#Unrelated visuals distract from the purpose and can confuse the audience.
=C. They should guide attention and support what the speaker explains aloud.#Correct. Visuals and spoken explanation work together to help the audience understand.
~D. They should include every detail from the speech.#Too much detail can overload the audience. Visuals should focus attention on key ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
What should a communication portfolio introduction do?
{
~A. Hide the purpose until the reviewer guesses it.#This weakens communication. The introduction should guide the reviewer.
~B. List files without explaining why they matter.#A list alone does not show mastery. The reviewer needs purpose and context.
~C. Focus only on how many files are included.#Quantity does not prove communication growth. Purposeful selection matters.
=D. Explain the purpose, audience, organization, and skills the reviewer should notice.#Correct. This helps the reviewer understand how the portfolio demonstrates readiness.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which portfolio organization is strongest?
{
=A. Group artifacts by skill and include a reflection for each artifact.#Correct. This structure helps the reviewer understand both the evidence and the communication skill shown.
~B. Place artifacts randomly with no labels.#Random placement makes the portfolio harder to follow and weakens the evidence of skill.
~C. Remove reflections so the portfolio is shorter.#Reflections explain why artifacts matter. Shorter is not better if clarity is lost.
~D. Include only decorative pages.#Design may help presentation, but artifacts must demonstrate communication skills.
}
::SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_PRE_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which plan best synthesizes Unit 6 communication skills?
{
~A. Choose a format first and think about audience later.#This reverses strong planning. Audience and purpose should guide format choices.
=B. Use evidence, audience awareness, digital format, and speaking organization together.#Correct. Unit synthesis requires combining multiple communication skills in one plan.
~C. Avoid research so the message sounds simple.#A simple message can still use evidence. Research supports professional recommendations.
~D. Make one message for every audience without revision.#This ignores audience needs. Synthesis includes adapting communication to context.
}
